==Abstract==

A compact binary record format for publishing structured data in handles.

==Motivation==

Spaces handles need a standard way to associate structured metadata — payment addresses, cryptographic identities, contact information — with a given name. Without a common wire format, each application would invent its own encoding, leading to fragmentation and interoperability failures. This SIP defines a minimal, extensible binary record format that fits within the constraints of on-chain covenant data while remaining easy to parse and extend.

==Specification==

===Wire Format===

A record set is a concatenation of records:

<pre>
+------------+------------+--- - -
| Record 0   | Record 1   | ...
+------------+------------+--- - -
</pre>

Each record:

<pre>
+---------+----------------+-----------+
|  RType  |  RData Length  |   RData   |
| 1 byte  |  CompactSize   |  variable |
+---------+----------------+-----------+
</pre>

'''RType''': Values <code>0x00</code>–<code>0xFE</code> are usable. <code>0xFF</code> is reserved.

'''RData Length''': Bitcoin CompactSize encoding (<code>0x00</code>–<code>0xFC</code> = 1 byte, <code>0xFD</code> = 3 bytes, <code>0xFE</code> = 5 bytes, <code>0xFF</code> = 9 bytes, all little-endian).

'''RData''': Record-type-specific payload.

Unknown types MUST be skipped using the length field and MUST be preserved when rewriting. Malformed data MUST cause the entire record set to be rejected.

===Record Types===

====SEQ — Sequence Record====

A monotonically increasing version number for the record set. Used by off-chain protocols to determine which version of a record set is newer. Higher values are newer; there is no wraparound arithmetic.

RType: <code>0x00</code>

<pre>
+-------------------+
|     version       |
|   CompactSize     |
+-------------------+
</pre>

The version is encoded as a CompactSize integer, supporting values from 0 to 2<sup>64</sup>-1. Small sequence numbers (0–252) require only a single byte of payload.

A record set MUST contain at most one SEQ record. If present, it MUST be the first record. Implementations MUST reject record sets containing a SEQ record in any other position or containing more than one SEQ record.

====TXT — Text Record====

A key-value pair where both key and value are UTF-8 strings.

RType: <code>0x01</code>

<pre>
+------------+---------------+-------------------+
|  key_len   |     key       |      value        |
|  1 byte    |  key_len B    |  remaining bytes  |
+------------+---------------+-------------------+
</pre>

The value length is derived from the data: <code>data_length - 1 - key_len</code>. A zero-length value is valid.

====BLOB — Binary Record====

Identical layout to TXT, but the value is raw bytes instead of UTF-8.

RType: <code>0x02</code>

<pre>
+------------+---------------+-------------------+
|  key_len   |     key       |      value        |
|  1 byte    |  key_len B    |  remaining bytes  |
+------------+---------------+-------------------+
</pre>

When serialized to JSON, BLOB values SHOULD be encoded as base64.

====Reserved====

Types <code>0x03</code>–<code>0xFE</code> are available for future use. Type <code>0xFF</code> is reserved as an extension prefix — if the 254 single-byte types are exhausted, a future SIP MAY define <code>0xFF</code> as an escape byte followed by additional type bytes to extend the type space.

Duplicate keys within the same record type SHOULD be rejected.

===Key Format (TXT and BLOB)===

Keys in TXT and BLOB records are case-sensitive and MUST consist of lowercase ASCII letters, digits, and hyphens (<code>a-z</code>, <code>0-9</code>, <code>-</code>). Implementations MUST reject TXT and BLOB records with keys containing uppercase letters or other characters outside this set. This ensures unambiguous key matching across implementations.

===Recommended TXT Records===

====Spaces Protocol====

{| class="wikitable"
! Key !! Value !! Example
|-
| space || Top Level Space Name || <code>@space</code>
|-
| handle || Subspace Name Handle || <code>user@space</code>
|}

====Payment Addresses====

{| class="wikitable"
! Key !! Value !! Example
|-
| btc || Bitcoin address || <code>bc1q...</code>
|-
| eth || Ethereum address || <code>0x...</code>
|-
| ln || BOLT 12 offer || <code>lno1...</code>
|}

====Identity & Keys====

{| class="wikitable"
! Key !! Value !! Example
|-
| nostr || Nostr public key || <code>npub1...</code>
|-
| tor || v3 onion address || <code>pg6mm...d.onion</code>
|-
| ssh || SSH public key || <code>ssh-ed25519 AAAA...</code>
|-
| pgp || PGP fingerprint (hex) || <code>3e7ba00a1b47...</code>
|-
| age || age encryption public key || <code>age1...</code>
|-
| did || Decentralized Identifier || <code>did:key:z6Mk...</code>
|-
| hyper || HyperDHT public key (hex) || <code>a1b2c3...</code>
|-
| bep44 || BEP 44 public key (hex) || <code>d4e5f6...</code>
|}

====General====

{| class="wikitable"
! Key !! Value !! Example
|-
| website || URL || <code>https://example.com</code>
|-
| email || Email address || <code>alice@example.com</code>
|-
| matrix || Matrix user ID || <code>@alice:matrix.org</code>
|-
| xmpp || XMPP address || <code>alice@jabber.org</code>
|}

===JSON Representation===

<pre>
[
  { "type": "seq", "version": 1 },
  { "type": "txt", "key": "space", "value": "@space" },
  { "type": "txt", "key": "handle", "value": "user@space" },
  { "type": "txt", "key": "nostr", "value": "npub1abc...xyz" },
  { "type": "txt", "key": "tor", "value": "pg6mm...d.onion" },
  { "type": "txt", "key": "ssh", "value": "ssh-ed25519 AAAA..." },
  { "type": "txt", "key": "btc", "value": "bc1q..." },
  { "type": "txt", "key": "website", "value": "https://example.com" },
  { "type": "txt", "key": "email", "value": "alice@example.com" },
  { "type": "blob", "key": "some-data", "value": "iVBORw0KGgo..." },
  { "type": "unknown", "rtype": 42, "rdata": "SGVsbG8gV29ybGQ..." }
]
</pre>

The <code>type</code> field MUST be lowercase (<code>"seq"</code>, <code>"txt"</code>, <code>"blob"</code>, <code>"unknown"</code>). BLOB values and unknown record data are base64 encoded in JSON.

==Rationale==

The format uses string-keyed TXT and BLOB records rather than assigning a numeric type to each use case. Recommended keys use standard community text encodings (e.g. <code>npub1...</code> for Nostr, <code>bc1q...</code> for Bitcoin) rather than raw binary because these encodings are self-describing and version-safe — a <code>bc1q</code> prefix distinguishes segwit v0 from <code>bc1p</code> (v1) without out-of-band context.

Bitcoin CompactSize is used for both the data length field and the SEQ version, keeping the common case (small values) to a single overhead byte while supporting the full <code>u64</code> range.

Type <code>0xFF</code> is reserved as an extension prefix so that the single-byte type space can be expanded in the future without breaking existing parsers.

==Backwards Compatibility==

This is a new format with no prior deployed version. Implementations that encounter unknown record types MUST skip them using the length field, ensuring forward compatibility as new types are defined.

==Reference Implementation==

<pre>
const TYPE_SEQ: u8 = 0x00;
const TYPE_TXT: u8 = 0x01;
const TYPE_BLOB: u8 = 0x02;

#[derive(Debug)]
pub enum RecordData {
    Seq(u64),
    Txt { key: String, value: String },
    Blob { key: String, value: Vec<u8> },
    Unknown { rtype: u8, rdata: Vec<u8> },
}

/// Record set iterator.
///
/// ```
/// for record in RecordIter::new(data) {
///     match record {
///         Ok(RecordData::Seq(version)) => println!("seq: {version}"),
///         Ok(RecordData::Txt { key, value }) => println!("{key}: {value}"),
///         Ok(RecordData::Blob { key, value }) => println!("{key}: ({} bytes)", value.len()),
///         Ok(RecordData::Unknown { rtype, .. }) => println!("unknown type: 0x{rtype:02x}"),
///         Err(e) => eprintln!("parse error: {e}"),
///     }
/// }
/// ```
pub struct RecordIter<'a> {
    data: &'a [u8],
    pos: usize,
    index: usize,
    seen_seq: bool,
}

impl<'a> RecordIter<'a> {
    pub fn new(data: &'a [u8]) -> Self {
        Self { data, pos: 0, index: 0, seen_seq: false }
    }
}

impl<'a> Iterator for RecordIter<'a> {
    type Item = Result<RecordData, &'static str>;

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        if self.pos >= self.data.len() { return None; }

        let t = self.data[self.pos];
        self.pos += 1;

        let len = match read_compact_size(self.data, &mut self.pos) {
            Ok(v) => v as usize,
            Err(e) => return Some(Err(e)),
        };
        if self.pos + len > self.data.len() { return Some(Err("data overflow")); }
        let rdata = &self.data[self.pos..self.pos + len];
        self.pos += len;

        let record = match t {
            TYPE_SEQ => {
                if self.seen_seq { return Some(Err("duplicate seq")); }
                if self.index > 0 { return Some(Err("seq must be first")); }
                self.seen_seq = true;
                let mut rpos = 0;
                match read_compact_size(rdata, &mut rpos) {
                    Ok(v) => Ok(RecordData::Seq(v)),
                    Err(e) => Err(e),
                }
            }
            TYPE_TXT => parse_kv(rdata).and_then(|(k, v)| {
                let v = std::str::from_utf8(v).map_err(|_| "invalid value")?;
                Ok(RecordData::Txt { key: k, value: v.to_string() })
            }),
            TYPE_BLOB => parse_kv(rdata).map(|(k, v)| {
                RecordData::Blob { key: k, value: v.to_vec() }
            }),
            _ => Ok(RecordData::Unknown { rtype: t, rdata: rdata.to_vec() }),
        };

        self.index += 1;
        Some(record)
    }
}

// -- helpers --

fn parse_kv(data: &[u8]) -> Result<(String, &[u8]), &'static str> {
    if data.is_empty() { return Err("empty data"); }
    let kl = data[0] as usize;
    if 1 + kl > data.len() { return Err("key length exceeds data"); }
    let key = std::str::from_utf8(&data[1..1 + kl]).map_err(|_| "invalid key")?;
    if !key.bytes().all(|b| matches!(b, b'a'..=b'z' | b'0'..=b'9' | b'-')) {
        return Err("invalid key: must be lowercase ascii, digits, or hyphens");
    }
    Ok((key.to_string(), &data[1 + kl..]))
}

fn read_compact_size(data: &[u8], pos: &mut usize) -> Result<u64, &'static str> {
    if *pos >= data.len() { return Err("unexpected eof"); }
    let first = data[*pos];
    *pos += 1;
    match first {
        0x00..=0xFC => Ok(first as u64),
        0xFD => {
            if *pos + 2 > data.len() { return Err("unexpected eof"); }
            let v = u16::from_le_bytes([data[*pos], data[*pos + 1]]);
            *pos += 2; Ok(v as u64)
        }
        0xFE => {
            if *pos + 4 > data.len() { return Err("unexpected eof"); }
            let v = u32::from_le_bytes(data[*pos..*pos + 4].try_into().unwrap());
            *pos += 4; Ok(v as u64)
        }
        0xFF => {
            if *pos + 8 > data.len() { return Err("unexpected eof"); }
            let v = u64::from_le_bytes(data[*pos..*pos + 8].try_into().unwrap());
            *pos += 8; Ok(v)
        }
    }
}
